{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:693c559e842aadf93ddcaba5f140c97aac1419c23fda3c1038c7a690c13b1213", "sample_idx": 2, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Section 1\nThe Guardian has issued corrections to several articles that were published recently. The corrections address various errors that were present in the original articles.\n\n*** \nSection 2\nThe corrections include clarifying that a new law making the abduction of a cat or dog a specific criminal offence applies to England and Northern Ireland, not the entire UK. Other corrections include rectifying the founders of Fertifa, a company that provides reproductive health benefit schemes to businesses. Some of the other corrections made are listed below:\n* The Guardian incorrectly referred to Eileen Burbidge as the founder of Fertifa; the actual founders are Tony Chen and Nick Kuan.\n* The actor Juliet Stevenson was misquoted as saying she worked with director \"Lowther\" instead of Alex Lawther.\n\nSection 3\nThe corrections also addressed errors in articles about Venetia Stanley's relationship with Herbert Asquith, where it was incorrectly stated that her father converted to Islam, when in fact it was her uncle. Several other articles were also amended, although the specific corrections were not detailed.\n\nSection 4\nReaders can contact the Readers' Editor to report any errors or concerns they may have. \nP.P.S The corrections demonstrate the Guardian's commitment to accuracy and transparency in their reporting.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Section", "logprob": -1.7165990357170813e-05}, {"token": " ", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "\n", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "The", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Guardian", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": " has", "logprob": -0.13147591054439545}, {"token": " issued", "logprob": -0.00184925168287009}, {"token": " corrections", "logprob"